            <summary>The retained device with updated metadata.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.SnapshotComplete">
            <summary>Marks the end of the initial snapshot replay.</summary>
            <remarks>Emitted once per subscription that starts while the monitor is running, after the initial replay and
            including when the replay is empty. A subscription started after disposal ends without any notification.</remarks>
            <param name="Revision">The revision of the replayed snapshot.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.SnapshotComplete.#ctor(System.Int64)">
            <summary>Marks the end of the initial snapshot replay.</summary>
            <remarks>Emitted once per subscription that starts while the monitor is running, after the initial replay and
            including when the replay is empty. A subscription started after disposal ends without any notification.</remarks>
            <param name="Revision">The revision of the replayed snapshot.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceChangeFeed">
            <summary>
            Holds the current physical-device snapshot, its revision, and independent bounded subscriptions.
            Subscribers receive initial membership, a completion marker, and subsequent ordered changes.
            Only one caller may apply scans, sequentially; applying after completion or failure throws.
            Call Complete only after the producer has stopped, or Fault to terminate monitoring unexpectedly.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceChangeFeed.Apply(System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey})">
            <summary>Applies one complete observed scan; throws if monitoring has ended.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceChangeFeed.Subscribe(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>Replays membership then streams changes; cancellation, overflow, or failure ends this subscription.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceChangeFeed.Fault(System.Exception)">
            <summary>Records the first terminal failure and faults subscriptions with its wrapped error.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceChangeFeed.Complete">
            <summary>Ends subscriptions normally after the producer has stopped; later subscriptions end empty.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry">
            <summary>
                Process-wide ownership coordinator keyed by stable member interface IDs. A physical-device connection
                claims all known member IDs; discovery takes one nonblocking per-interface lease.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    The lease belongs to the CONNECTION, not to a session: it is acquired before physical connection
                    creation and released if creation fails or when that connection is disposed. Sessions come and go
                    over a connection without touching it.
                </para>
                <para>
                    A grouped physical YubiKey admits one live connection across CCID, FIDO HID, and OTP HID. A second
                    acquisition through any known member is refused immediately with
                    <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionInUseException" />; it never waits for an unbounded holder to end.
                </para>
                <para>
                    Discovery holds its exclusive lease across physical connect, device-info exchange, and connection
                    disposal. Waiting connections have priority over a later discovery attempt so repeated scans cannot
                    starve a session.
                </para>
                <para>
                    LIMITATION: in-process only. A different process holding the card can still interfere; that is
                    outside what this registry can see. Keyed by the PER-INTERFACE DeviceId (reader name / HID path
                    based), which is stable across scans while the device stays plugged, so registrations made through
                    devices from one scan are visible to readers created in later scans. For a composite,
                    <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.ResolveInterfaceId(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType)" /> resolves the requested connection to a member instead of using
                    the composite DeviceId, which names the evidence tier and is not stable across scans. Standalone
                    devices use their own DeviceId. Idle coordinator entries are retained
                    for the process lifetime: this is bounded by unique interface IDs observed and avoids unsafe
                    remove/recreate races between lease acquisition and dictionary eviction.
                </para>
                <para>
                    Exclusion requires an overlapping member ID. YubiKeys from disjoint scoped scans (for example
                    CCID-only and FIDO-only) have disjoint claims, even if they report the same serial or topology key.
                    No global physical-identity alias is registered; callers must coordinate such YubiKeys themselves.
                </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.IsInUse(System.String)">
            <summary>Whether this process currently holds at least one live connection to the interface.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.IsInterfaceInUse(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType)">
            <summary>
                Whether the interface of <paramref name="device" /> that would serve <paramref name="connection" />
                is in use. Published-device slots are resolved through
                <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.YubiKeyDevice.TryResolveSlot(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IYubiKeyConnectionSlot@)" />, the same routing a connect uses, so the check
                matches the interface a read would actually open.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.ResolveInterfaceId(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType)">
            <summary>
                Resolves the member interface ID serving <paramref name="connection" />. Discovery uses this to
                coordinate one interface; grouped public connections acquire the complete member lease scope.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.AcquireConnectionAsync(System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyCollection{System.String},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
                Acquires every known interface lease for one physical YubiKey as a single logical registration,
                before physical connection creation. Interface ids are de-duplicated and acquired in ordinal
                order; partial acquisition rolls back in reverse order. Waits while discovery owns an interface;
                cancellation applies only while waiting. Standalone devices pass a one-element scope.
            </summary>
            <exception cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionInUseException">Any member ID already has a live connection.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.TryAcquireDiscovery(System.String,System.IDisposable)">
            <summary>
                Attempts to acquire exclusive discovery ownership without waiting. Returns <c>null</c> while any
                connection owns or is already waiting for the interface. Throws a fresh
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.UnrecoveredConnectionException" /> when an earlier native owner has not proven release.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.IsDiscoveryAvailable(System.String)">
            <summary>
                Nonblocking precheck for <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.TryAcquireDiscovery(System.String,System.IDisposable)" />: whether discovery could take the
                interface right now, meaning no discovery lease, live connection, or waiting connection holds it.
                An interface this process has never registered is available. The answer can change before the
                caller acts on it; only <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.TryAcquireDiscovery(System.String,System.IDisposable)" /> claims the interface atomically.
            </summary>
            <exception cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.UnrecoveredConnectionException">
                An earlier native owner of the interface has not proven release.
            </exception>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.HasConnections">
            <summary>At least one live connection lease is held.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.IsDiscoveryAvailable">
            <summary>No discovery lease, live connection, or waiting connection holds this interface.</summary>
            <exception cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.UnrecoveredConnectionException">An earlier native owner has not proven release.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.Claim(System.String)">
            <summary>Takes the lease, or refuses when the interface is already held.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.MarkUnrecovered(System.Exception)">
            <summary>
                Records that native work on this interface has not proven release. Later acquisitions throw
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.UnrecoveredConnectionException" />. If a discovery lease is held, the state clears when
                that lease is released (its native teardown finished late) and a connection already waiting for
                the lease fails now; otherwise it lasts for the process. The first failure is kept.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.WaitForRecovery">
            <summary>Completes when a discovery-lease release clears an unrecovered state; already complete if none.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.TryAcquireDiscovery(System.IDisposable)">
            <summary>Takes the discovery lease without waiting, or returns <c>null</c> while anything else holds or awaits the interface.</summary>
            <exception cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.UnrecoveredConnectionException">An earlier native owner has not proven release.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.InterfaceOwnership.Release(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.LeaseKind)">
            <summary>
                Returns one lease. Releasing the discovery lease also clears an unrecovered state and wakes
                waiting connections.
            </summary>
            <exception cref="T:System.InvalidOperationException">No matching lease is held.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.MarkUnrecovered(System.IDisposable,System.Exception)">
            <summary>Marks the interfaces behind a registration from this registry as unrecovered.</summary>
            <exception cref="T:System.InvalidCastException"><paramref name="registration" /> did not come from this registry.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.TryMarkUnrecovered(System.IDisposable,System.Exception)">
            <summary>Like <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceConnectionRegistry.MarkUnrecovered(System.IDisposable,System.Exception)" />, but ignores registrations from elsewhere.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceFlags">
            <summary>
                Miscellaneous flags representing various settings available on the YubiKey.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceFlags.None">
            <summary>
                No device flags are set.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceFlags.RemoteWakeup">
            <summary>
                USB remote wakeup is enabled.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceFlags.TouchEject">
            <summary>
                The CCID touch-eject feature is enabled.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    For the CCID connection, the YubiKey behaves as a smart card reader and smart
                    card. When this flag is disabled, the smart card is always present in the smart
                    card reader. When enabled, the smart card will be ejected by default,
                    and the user is required to touch the YubiKey to insert the smart card. For
                    this to take effect, all <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceCapabilities" /> which do not depend
                    on the CCID connection (such as <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceCapabilities.Fido2" />,
                    <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceCapabilities.U2f" />, and <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceCapabilities.Otp" />)
                    must be disabled.
                </para>
                <para>
                    To automatically eject the smart card following a touch, see
                    <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo.AutoEjectTimeout" />.
                </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo.Equals(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo)">
            <summary>Compares every field by value, including the bytes of <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo.ChallengeResponseTimeout"/>.</summary>
            <remarks>
                The compiler-generated record equality compares <see cref="T:System.ReadOnlyMemory`1"/> by backing reference, so two
                snapshots decoded from separate response buffers would never be equal. Discovery relies on this comparison
                to publish <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceUpdated"/> only when metadata actually changed.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo.CreateFromTlvs(System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyCollection{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Utilities.Tlv},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion)">
            <summary>Decodes device-information TLVs, ignoring unknown tags.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Absent optional fields use zero or null defaults. A supplied firmware version takes precedence
                over tag 0x05, but a present firmware field must still have a valid encoding.
            </remarks>
            <exception cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.BadResponseException">A known field is malformed or no firmware version is available.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfoReader">
            <summary>
                Reads read-only <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo" /> from a YubiKey over any supported transport.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                This is Core-owned read-only discovery logic. It is used by composite-device discovery and is
                shared with the Management module, which delegates its device-info read to this reader.
                Mutating Management operations (configuration writes, set mode, reset) are owned by Management.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfoReader.ReadAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IProtocol,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
                Reads the full multi-page device info from the device over the supplied protocol.
            </summary>
            <param name="protocol">
                A Core protocol over SmartCard, FIDO HID, or OTP HID. The reader does not own or dispose it.
            </param>
            <param name="defaultVersion">
                Optional fallback firmware version passed to <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo.CreateFromTlvs(System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyCollection{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Utilities.Tlv},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion)" />. When
                <c>null</c>, the firmware version is derived from the device-info TLVs.
            </param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token.</param>
            <returns>The parsed <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo" />.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryBudgets">
            <summary>
                Bounds discovery callers' waits, not native work. Abandoned work completes and cleans up on its own;
                callers need not clean it up. Accessing these budgets does not throw.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryBudgets.ScanUpperBound">
            <summary>The combined wait budget for a scan's enumeration, identity, and metadata stages.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader">
            <summary>Bounds each caller's wait for a shared discovery device-info read.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader.ReadBoundedAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.TimeSpan,Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Boolean,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope)">
            <summary>
                Opens a short-lived connection over the given interface and reads <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo" />,
                bounded by a hard wall-clock budget.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    The budget bounds the caller's <em>wait</em>, not the work: an in-flight native call (e.g.
                    <c>SCardTransmit</c> against a card busy with a long applet operation such as RSA key
                    generation) cannot observe cancellation. On budget exhaustion the read is therefore
                    <em>abandoned, not aborted</em> — this method throws <see cref="T:System.TimeoutException" /> so the
                    scan can proceed, while the abandoned task keeps running in the background and disposes its
                    protocol through <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ProtocolDeviceInfo.ReadAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IConnection,System.Threading.CancellationToken)" /> and its discovery-owned connection through
                    <c>SharedDeviceInfoRead.ConnectAndReadAsync</c> when the native call eventually returns.
                </para>
                <para>
                    External cancellation via <paramref name="cancellationToken" /> likewise abandons only that
                    caller's wait (propagating <see cref="T:System.OperationCanceledException" />). The shared operation's
                    lifetime is independent of every waiter and remains the single in-flight read for its stable
                    interface/connection key until it completes.
                </para>
            </remarks>
            <exception cref="T:System.TimeoutException">The budget elapsed before the read completed.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryIdentityReader">
            <summary>
                Reads read-only <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo" /> (including the serial number) from a single per-interface
                device, used to disambiguate multiple same-PID physical keys during composite discovery.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                This opens a short-lived connection over the interface and reads device info via the Core-owned
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader" />, with a hard per-attempt wall-clock budget so a card that is busy
                with a long applet operation (e.g. RSA key generation) cannot stall the scan — the attempt is
                abandoned, not aborted (see <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader.ReadBoundedAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.TimeSpan,Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Boolean,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope)" />). It retries transient
                PC/SC failures (e.g. sharing violations) because, unlike the metadata read, a correct serial here is
                required to tell two same-model keys apart; a budget timeout is NOT retried because it indicates a
                busy card, and retrying would only extend the stall. Any failure is swallowed and reported as
                <c>null</c> so discovery degrades to conservative no-merge rather than aborting. It uses only Core
                primitives and introduces no dependency on Management.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadAdmission">
            <summary>
                Serializes potentially sibling USB discovery reads before worker admission. These fixed PID gates
                contain no identity or metadata; distinct finders and evidence epochs share only native admission.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope">
            <summary>Owns finder-scoped single-flight reads and transport epochs.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope.Advance">
            <summary>
                Signals that hotplug activity was observed, detaching future device-info reads from any
                in-flight read started against the previous physical topology, and failing superseded reads
                that have not yet opened their interface (including reads still queued for worker admission,
                whose wait is cancelled so they cannot accumulate behind hung workers). Called from the same
                place <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys" /> invalidates its identity/metadata caches.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.TransportEpoch">
            <summary>
                One immutable hotplug epoch: a generation and the cancellation source that fires when this
                epoch is superseded. The retired CTS is only cancelled, never disposed, because detached
                waiters may still be observing it (a cancelled timer-less CTS is plain collectible garbage).
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.TransportEpoch.#ctor(System.Int64)">
            <summary>
                One immutable hotplug epoch: a generation and the cancellation source that fires when this
                epoch is superseded. The retired CTS is only cancelled, never disposed, because detached
                waiters may still be observing it (a cancelled timer-less CTS is plain collectible garbage).
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.TransportEpoch.Superseded">
            <summary>Fires when this epoch has been replaced by newer transport activity.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadKey">
            <summary>Identifies one interface/connection read within a transport generation.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadKey.#ctor(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.Int64)">
            <summary>Identifies one interface/connection read within a transport generation.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause">
            <summary>
                Why a best-effort discovery device-info read was skipped. Carried on
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkippedException" /> so logs and diagnostics can distinguish unsupported
                discovery access, active connection ownership, worker-admission saturation, and transport
                supersession.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause.NoDiscoveryProvider">
            <summary>The device does not expose the internal discovery-only connection path.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause.InterfaceLeaseHeld">
            <summary>
                A connection in this process owns (or is waiting for) the interface; discovery must not clobber it.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause.WorkerAdmissionSaturated">
            <summary>
                All bounded discovery workers were busy and this read path skips instead of waiting
                (the best-effort metadata path; identity reads wait for a slot instead).
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause.SupersededByTransportActivity">
            <summary>
                Hotplug activity superseded this read before it opened the interface: the physical topology it
                was started against may no longer exist, so it aborts (or its queued admission wait is
                cancelled) instead of reading hardware it can no longer name. Retried on the next scan.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkippedException">
            <summary>
                Thrown when a best-effort discovery device-info read is intentionally skipped because the target does
                not support the internal discovery-only connection path, an active or waiting connection prevents
                exclusive access, bounded worker admission is saturated, or transport activity superseded the read
                before it opened the interface (see <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader.ReadBoundedAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.TimeSpan,Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Boolean,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope)" />). The read does
                not open a normal session connection or transmit. Callers degrade like any other failed best-effort
                read: identity unknown / try next transport.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkippedException.#ctor(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkipCause)">
            <summary>
                Thrown when a best-effort discovery device-info read is intentionally skipped because the target does
                not support the internal discovery-only connection path, an active or waiting connection prevents
                exclusive access, bounded worker admission is saturated, or transport activity superseded the read
                before it opened the interface (see <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryDeviceInfoReader.ReadBoundedAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IYubiKey,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.TimeSpan,Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Boolean,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadScope)" />). The read does
                not open a normal session connection or transmit. Callers degrade like any other failed best-effort
                read: identity unknown / try next transport.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryReadSkippedException.Cause">
            <summary>The specific reason the read was skipped.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryWorkerAdmission">
            <summary>
                Process-wide, nonqueueing admission for best-effort native discovery workers.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DiscoveryWorkerAdmission.AcquireAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
                Waits (asynchronously, unbounded) for a worker slot. Used by identity reads, whose callers bound
                their own wait via the read budget; the slot count still bounds concurrent native work.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DisposalGate">
            <summary>
                One-shot disposal with shared completion. The first caller wins and runs teardown. Every concurrent or
                later caller observes that same completion: async callers await it, sync callers block on it. Any disposal
                call returning therefore implies teardown actually finished, and all callers see the same outcome.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                When supplied, the lease is never released before inner teardown completes. The caller selects whether a
                failed teardown supplies enough release evidence to release the lease or must retain it as unrecovered.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DisposalGate.#ctor(System.IDisposable,System.Boolean,System.Action{System.Exception})">
            <summary>
                One-shot disposal with shared completion. The first caller wins and runs teardown. Every concurrent or
                later caller observes that same completion: async callers await it, sync callers block on it. Any disposal
                call returning therefore implies teardown actually finished, and all callers see the same outcome.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                When supplied, the lease is never released before inner teardown completes. The caller selects whether a
                failed teardown supplies enough release evidence to release the lease or must retain it as unrecovered.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DisposalGate.Dispose(System.Action)">
            <summary>Synchronous disposal. The winner tears down inline; a loser blocks on the winner's teardown.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DisposalGate.DisposeAsync(System.Func{System.Threading.Tasks.ValueTask})">
            <summary>Asynchronous disposal. The winner tears down asynchronously; a loser awaits that teardown.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IFindYubiKeys">
            <summary>Discovers connected YubiKeys. Implemented by <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys"/>; fake it to test code that discovers keys.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IFindYubiKeys.FindAllAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>Runs one discovery scan and returns the YubiKeys that expose any of the requested connections.</summary>
            <param name="type">
                Result filter applied after interfaces are grouped into YubiKeys; <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType.Hid"/>
                matches FIDO or OTP and <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType.Unknown"/> returns nothing.
            </param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancels discovery waits, not already-dispatched native work.</param>
            <returns>The matching YubiKeys, in unspecified order, or an empty list.</returns>
            <exception cref="T:System.OperationCanceledException"><paramref name="cancellationToken"/> was canceled.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys">
            <summary>
                Discovers physical YubiKeys with bounded native enumeration and read waits. Await scans and pass a
                cancellation token to cancel their waits. Enumeration timeouts omit only the affected transport;
                caller cancellation throws <see cref="T:System.OperationCanceledException" />, and other enumeration failures propagate.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.SmartCard.IFindPcscDevices,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.IFindHidInterfaces,System.Func{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IDevice,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IYubiKeyConnectionSlot},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IDeviceTopologyResolver,System.Nullable{System.TimeSpan})">
            <summary>Creates a finder limited to <paramref name="interfaces"/>. Callers must retire its read scope when discovery ends.</summary>
            <param name="findPcscService">Enumerator required when <paramref name="interfaces"/> includes smart cards.</param>
            <param name="findHidService">Enumerator required when <paramref name="interfaces"/> includes HID.</param>
            <param name="createSlot">Creates a connection slot for each included interface.</param>
            <param name="interfaces">Interfaces to enumerate and probe; <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType.Hid"/> expands to FIDO and OTP.</param>
            <param name="topologyResolver">Optional platform topology resolver.</param>
            <param name="enumerationBudget">Maximum wait per transport; defaults to the discovery enumeration budget.</param>
            <exception cref="T:System.ArgumentNullException">An enumerator required by <paramref name="interfaces"/> is missing.</exception>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.CachedIdentity">
            <summary>A successful identity read plus the evidence context that makes it reusable.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.CachedIdentity.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.DeviceInfo,System.Nullable{System.UInt16})">
            <summary>A successful identity read plus the evidence context that makes it reusable.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.FindAllAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.ResolveTopologyKey(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IDevice,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType)">
            <summary>
                Best-effort tier-1 topology read. Never throws and never blocks meaningfully: a failure is a
                debug log and a null key, which drops the interface to the unchanged serial/PID tiers.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.Create">
            <summary>
                Creates a finder over every interface (smart card, FIDO HID, OTP HID) using this platform's PC/SC and
                HID backends. Prefer <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.YubiKeyManager.FindAllAsync(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType,System.Threading.CancellationToken)"/> unless code needs an injectable
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.IFindYubiKeys"/>.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FindYubiKeys.CreateForInterfaces(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionType)">
            <summary>Creates a finder that never constructs the backend of an excluded transport.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.IsAlphaOrBeta">
            <summary>
                Gets a value indicating whether this firmware version represents an alpha or beta YubiKey.
                Alpha/beta keys report firmware versions with major version 0 but should be treated as the latest version
                for feature compatibility checks.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    Treat an alpha or beta version as newer than any released firmware, and as being at least 5.8.0.
                    Every alpha and beta key is built from a development branch at or ahead of that release, so a
                    feature gate must not refuse one. <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.IsAtLeast(System.Int32,System.Int32,System.Int32)" /> already returns
                    <see langword="true" /> for these versions, and
                    <c>Feature.IsSupportedByFirmware</c> short-circuits on this property, so the usual gates need no
                    special handling. Do not write <c>!IsAlphaOrBeta &amp;&amp; IsAtLeast(...)</c>: that cancels the
                    built-in allowance and downgrades a development key to legacy behavior.
                </para>
                <para>
                    The version carries no finer detail. Different development builds report the same placeholder
                    (typically 0.0.1), so it cannot distinguish one build from another. An individual applet build can
                    therefore lack a feature this property implies, usually because the applet was cut before the
                    instruction landed. The device reports that itself, as <c>SW=0x6D00</c> (instruction not supported)
                    or a comparable rejection.
                </para>
                <para>
                    That case is deliberately not handled in code. Detecting it would mean either abandoning the
                    optimistic assumption, which breaks every correctly built development key, or probing the device
                    before each gated call, which costs a round trip to accommodate a defective build. A test failing
                    against a development key with <c>SW=0x6D00</c> is the expected signal: the applet on that specific
                    key is missing the instruction. Check the key before suspecting the gate or the command encoding.
                </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.Default">
            <summary>
                A default firmware version (0.0.0), representing unknown or uninitialized version.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.CompareTo(System.Object)">
            <summary>
                Compares the relative sort order of the specified object to the current object.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                By definition any object compares greater than <see langword="null" />.
            </remarks>
            <returns>
                An integer that indicates whether the current instance precedes (negative value),
                follows (positive value), or occurs in the same position (0) in the sort order
                as the other object.
            </returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.CompareTo(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion)">
            <summary>
                Compares the relative sort order of the current instance with another object of
                the same type.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Alpha/beta keys (version 0.0.0) are treated as the latest version and will compare
                greater than any other version.
            </remarks>
            <returns>
                An integer that indicates whether the current instance precedes (negative value),
                follows (positive value), or occurs in the same position (0) in the sort order
                as the other object.
            </returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.FromString(System.String)">
            <summary>
                Parses a firmware version string in format "major.minor.patch".
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.FromSelectResponse(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>
                Extracts a firmware version from applet SELECT response text.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                SELECT response text is untrusted. Malformed or unexpected content returns <c>null</c> so callers
                can fall back to another version source.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion.FromBytes(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>
                Creates a <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion" /> from a byte array.
                The byte array must contain exactly three bytes, representing the major, minor, and patch versions.
            </summary>
            <param name="bytes">A byte array containing the version information.</param>
            <returns>A <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion" /> instance.</returns>
            <exception cref="T:System.ArgumentException">Thrown if the byte array does not contain exactly three bytes.</exception>
            <remarks>
                The first byte represents the major version, the second byte represents the minor version,
                and the third byte represents the patch version.
            </remarks>
        </member>
