        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.POLLNVAL">
            <summary>Invalid request: fd not open (only returned in revents; ignored in events).</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.EFD_CLOEXEC">
            <summary>Close the descriptor on exec.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.EFD_NONBLOCK">
            <summary>Open the event fd in non-blocking mode.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.EINTR">
            <summary>EINTR: interrupted system call.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.EAGAIN">
            <summary>EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK: no data available on a non-blocking descriptor.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.PollNative(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.PollFd[],System.UIntPtr,System.Int32)">
            <summary>
            Wait for events on the specified file descriptors.
            </summary>
            <param name="fds">Array of PollFd structures describing the file descriptors to monitor.</param>
            <param name="nfds">Number of items in the fds array.</param>
            <param name="timeout">Timeout in milliseconds. -1 means infinite, 0 means return immediately.</param>
            <returns>Number of file descriptors with events, 0 on timeout, -1 on error (check errno).</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LibcHelpers.GetErrnoString">
            <summary>
            Returns the native error message for the last failed P/Invoke. Call this immediately after
            the failing operation, before invoking other native or managed APIs.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.MacOS.HidInput.NativeMethods">
            <summary>
                Native entry points for the macOS persistent-input owner in <c>Yubico.NativeShims</c>.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.Cfgmgr32.NativeMethods.CmErrorCode">
            <summary>
                Configuration Manager CONFIGRET return status codes
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.HidD.WindowsHidReportAccess.OpenIOConnection">
            <summary>
                Opens the handle used for input/output reports (FIDO).
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Requires GENERIC_READ | GENERIC_WRITE because this path really does use ReadFile/WriteFile
                (see <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.HidD.WindowsHidReportAccess.GetInputReport" /> and <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.HidD.WindowsHidReportAccess.SetOutputReport(System.Byte[])" />), unlike the feature path.
                Do NOT add a fall back to a lesser access level here: a zero-access handle would open
                successfully and then fail on every subsequent ReadFile/WriteFile, turning one clear
                UnauthorizedAccessException at open time — which carries the elevation guidance a non-elevated
                Windows caller needs — into cryptic failures at each I/O call.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.HidD.WindowsHidReportAccess.OpenFeatureConnection">
            <summary>
                Opens the handle used for feature reports (OTP), with no desired access.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    OTP feature-report I/O uses HidD_GetFeature/HidD_SetFeature, which are IOCTLs that succeed on
                    a zero-access handle. The OTP interface is a keyboard top-level collection, and Windows
                    refuses GENERIC_READ/GENERIC_WRITE on the system keyboard even for an elevated process
                    (anti-keylogger restriction). Opening with no access sidesteps that while still permitting
                    feature-report IOCTLs.
                </para>
                <para>
                    Scope of the evidence, stated precisely: the constructor's metadata probe shows only that this
                    same path OPENS with zero access (CreateFile + HidD_GetPreparsedData/HidP_GetCaps). It does not
                    exercise HidD_GetFeature/HidD_SetFeature, so it is supporting evidence, not proof that feature
                    I/O succeeds. Sufficiency for feature I/O rests on the Win32 contract for these IOCTLs plus the
                    Windows hardware run recorded in docs/architecture/connection-ownership-and-contention.md (YubiOtp integration
                    10/10, fw 5.8.0).
                </para>
                <para>
                    <b>Why there is no "try read/write, fall back to none" retry, as hidapi does.</b> hidapi's
                    Windows backend (<c>windows/hid.c</c>, <c>hid_open_path</c>) opens with
                    GENERIC_READ | GENERIC_WRITE and, on failure, retries with zero access, commenting that system
                    devices such as keyboards cannot be opened read/write because the system takes exclusive
                    control to prevent keyloggers, but that feature reports still work. It needs that retry because
                    it returns ONE handle serving both <c>hid_read</c>/<c>hid_write</c> (ReadFile/WriteFile) and
                    <c>hid_get_feature_report</c> (IOCTL), so it must request the maximal access and degrade.
                </para>
                <para>
                    This type splits those into two handles instead — <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.HidD.WindowsHidReportAccess.OpenIOConnection" /> for
                    ReadFile/WriteFile, this method for feature IOCTLs — so each already requests exactly what its
                    callers use. Adding the retry here would buy nothing and cost a syscall: read/write access
                    grants no capability this path uses; on the keyboard-class OTP collection the first attempt
                    always fails, so it would always fall through to zero access anyway; and zero access already
                    succeeds in a superset of the cases read/write does, being the least restrictive request and
                    not subject to share-mode conflicts. There is no case a read/write-first retry could rescue
                    that this call does not already handle.
                </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.WinSCard.NativeMethods">
            <summary>
                Windows-only WinSCard entry points that the cross-platform <c>Yubico.NativeShims</c> layer does not
                expose. Bound directly against <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.Libraries.WinSCard" /> instead of through the shim,
                following the same direct-import pattern as Cfgmgr32 and HidD in this folder: a shared
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.Libraries" /> constant plus <c>DllImportSearchPath.System32</c>. These methods must only
                ever be called on Windows — .NET resolves the import lazily on first call, so merely referencing
                this type on macOS/Linux loads nothing.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Windows.WinSCard.NativeMethods.SCardGetReaderDeviceInstanceId(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Desktop.SCard.SCardContext,System.String,System.Char[],System.Int32@)">
            <summary>
                Maps a PC/SC reader name to the device instance ID of the reader's devnode (Windows 8+).
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Two-call pattern: pass a null buffer to learn the required character count, then call again with
                a buffer of that size. Returns <c>SCARD_S_SUCCESS</c> or an <c>SCARD_E_*</c> code (see
                <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Desktop.SCard.ErrorCode" />); <c>SCARD_E_UNKNOWN_READER</c> and
                <c>SCARD_E_READER_UNAVAILABLE</c> are ordinary outcomes mid-hotplug, not faults.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.CtapConstants">
            <summary>
            CTAP HID protocol constants as defined in the FIDO CTAP specification.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidConnection">
            <summary>
            Wraps a synchronous HID IO report connection for FIDO/CTAP communication.
            Provides async interface for 64-byte FIDO HID packets.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidConnection.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.IHidConnection)">
            <summary>
            Wraps a synchronous HID IO report connection for FIDO/CTAP communication.
            Provides async interface for 64-byte FIDO HID packets.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol">
            <summary>
            Implements FIDO CTAP HID protocol for communication with YubiKey FIDO interface.
            Supports CTAP HID framing, channel management, and YubiKey Management vendor commands.
            Based on FIDO CTAP HID Protocol Specification.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Concurrency: CTAP HID permits one transaction at a time per channel — a request is an init
                packet plus continuation packets, and a foreign init packet mid-transaction aborts it on the
                device. This class serializes full logical exchanges (including lazy channel initialization)
                through an internal guard: overlapping calls are refused immediately. An exchange in flight runs to
                completion.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection,Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol},System.Func{System.Int32,System.Byte[]})">
            <summary>
            Implements FIDO CTAP HID protocol for communication with YubiKey FIDO interface.
            Supports CTAP HID framing, channel management, and YubiKey Management vendor commands.
            Based on FIDO CTAP HID Protocol Specification.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Concurrency: CTAP HID permits one transaction at a time per channel — a request is an init
                packet plus continuation packets, and a foreign init packet mid-transaction aborts it on the
                device. This class serializes full logical exchanges (including lazy channel initialization)
                through an internal guard: overlapping calls are refused immediately. An exchange in flight runs to
                completion.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.Configure(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion,System.Nullable{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.SmartCard.Apdu.ProtocolConfiguration})">
            <inheritdoc cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Abstractions.IProtocol.Configure(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.FirmwareVersion,System.Nullable{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.SmartCard.Apdu.ProtocolConfiguration})" />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.InitializeAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidProtocol.InitializeAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)" />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.SendCommandAsync(System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.SendCommandAsync(System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Credentials.UserPresenceNotification,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidProtocol.SendCommandAsync(System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Credentials.UserPresenceNotification,System.Threading.CancellationToken)" />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.EnsureChannelInitializedAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Initializes the CTAP HID channel if not already done. Must be called from within the
            exchange guard (or single-threaded initialization) — the INIT handshake is itself an
            exchange that must not interleave with other traffic.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.AcquireCtapHidChannelAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Acquires a CTAP HID channel by sending CTAPHID_INIT to the broadcast channel.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.TransmitCommand(System.UInt32,System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Credentials.UserPresenceNotification,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Transmits a CTAP HID command and receives the response.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.SendRequest(System.UInt32,System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Sends a CTAP HID request with proper packet framing.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.ReceiveResponse(System.UInt32,System.Byte,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Credentials.UserPresenceNotification,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Receives a CTAP HID response, handling keep-alive and multi-packet responses.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
            <paramref name="exchangeToken"/> governs the reads and is uncancellable inside an
            exchange, so a partially framed response is never stranded. <paramref name="callerToken"/>
            is signal-only: it never aborts a read, it makes this method send CTAPHID_CANCEL.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.ConstructInitPacket(System.UInt32,System.Byte,System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte},System.Int32)">
            <summary>
            Constructs a CTAP HID initialization packet.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.ConstructContinuationPacket(System.UInt32,System.Byte,System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>
            Constructs a CTAP HID continuation packet.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.GetPacketCommand(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>
            Extracts the command byte from a packet, removing the init bit.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.GetPacketLength(System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>
            Extracts the payload length from an init packet.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.FidoHidProtocol.Dispose">
            <summary>
                Releases this protocol. The connection is NOT disposed: a protocol is a user of the connection it
                was handed, never its owner. Whoever created the connection disposes it.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection">
            <summary>
            A FIDO HID connection to a YubiKey using CTAP HID protocol (64-byte packets).
            Used for FIDO2/CTAP2, CTAP1/U2F, and Management over the FIDO interface.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                <para>
                    The physical FIDO HID interface admits exactly one live SDK connection and native HID handle.
                    A second connection attempt is refused with <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Devices.ConnectionInUseException" />
                    before the native interface is opened. Dispose the current connection before reopening it.
                </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection.PacketSize">
            <summary>
            Size of HID packets for FIDO/CTAP protocol (always 64 bytes).
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection.SendAsync(System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Sends a 64-byte HID packet to the YubiKey.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                This Tier 2 method bypasses <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Sessions.ApplicationSession" />,
                <c>ConnectionSessionGuard</c>, and <c>ExchangeGuard</c>. The caller owns CTAP HID
                framing, sequencing, response correlation, keep-alive handling, concurrency exclusion, and recovery.
                Do not interleave it with a live session or another raw operation; dispose and reopen when state is uncertain.
                Keep borrowed <paramref name="packet" /> memory valid until the returned task is terminal;
                only then zero sensitive caller-owned input. This applies to every implementation, even though
                built-in macOS output copies the packet. Built-in macOS rejects cancellation before dispatch;
                after dispatch output may succeed, and the task waits for native completion and resource drain.
                Cancellation alone does not make a raw connection reusable.
            </remarks>
            <param name="packet">The packet data (must be 64 bytes).</param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection.ReceiveAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Receives a 64-byte HID packet from the YubiKey.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                This Tier 2 method bypasses <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Sessions.ApplicationSession" />,
                <c>ConnectionSessionGuard</c>, and <c>ExchangeGuard</c>. Pair receives with the
                caller's own serialized send state. Built-in macOS cancellation of a pending read completes
                that read and frees its overlap slot; input remains active and later reports are queued.
                Cancellation does not abort an in-flight device protocol exchange. After interruption or
                interleaving, dispose and reopen the connection; await disposal for native drain. Cancellation
                alone does not make a raw protocol exchange reusable.
            </remarks>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token.</param>
            <returns>The received packet (64 bytes).</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidProtocol">
            <summary>
            Protocol interface for FIDO HID communication using CTAP HID framing.
            Supports CTAP HID channel initialization and YubiKey Management vendor commands.
            </summary>
            <remarks>
                Implementations admit one full logical exchange at a time. An overlapping operation throws
                <see cref="T:System.InvalidOperationException" /> immediately. A token is checked before entry; an exchange already
                in flight runs to completion to avoid stranding the device mid-transaction.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidProtocol.InitializeAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>
            Initializes the CTAP HID channel if it has not already been initialized.
            </summary>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidProtocol.SendCommandAsync(System.Byte,System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>Sends a raw CTAP HID command without an SDK user-presence notification.</summary>
            <param name="command">The CTAPHID command byte, not a CTAP2 operation code.</param>
            <param name="data">The command payload.</param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancellation token.</param>
