            </exception>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadReactor">
            <summary>Internal readiness mechanics, not an application callback/event API.</summary>
            <remarks>
            One lazy poller serves all live registrations, exits when idle, and recreates its eventfd
            on the next admission. DisposeAsync permanently shuts down the instance. Open/write/ioctl
            and checked device close belong to the connection's native owner, never this poller.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadReactor.Register(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.Int64,System.Action{System.Int64,System.Int16},System.Action{System.Exception})">
            <summary>Every published admission returns a retained token, including a failed activation.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Generation is immutable and supplied to every readiness callback. Callbacks must be
            bounded internal mechanics (for example one nonblocking TryRead), never application
            code or synchronous close. Complete future consumer TCSs with RunContinuationsAsynchronously.
            POLLERR/HUP/NVAL and callback exceptions remove the registration and notify onTerminal
            once. Terminal-notification exceptions cannot escape into sibling registrations.
            Activation reports the immutable admission outcome (null on success), independently
            from actual Quiescence. Exceptions from Register occur only before publication.
            Admission while the local wake fd is retiring waits in Activation on that same poller;
            no replacement wake fd/thread or cleanup job is created before retirement finishes.
            </remarks>
            <param name="handle">Owner's live descriptor; a separate SafeHandle reference is retained until explicit release after drain.</param>
            <param name="generation">Immutable owner generation returned with readiness, in addition to the unique token identity.</param>
            <param name="onReady">Bounded internal nonblocking input mechanics, receiving generation and native readiness flags.</param>
            <param name="onTerminal">Contained internal terminal notification; its return is included in actual quiescence.</param>
            <returns>The published identity, even when activation fails; retain it through quiescence and reference release.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration">
            <summary>A unique registration identity paired with one immutable connection generation.</summary>
            <remarks>
            RequestRemoval publishes control before signaling and returns any wake failure, not proof
            of removal. Quiescence never faults: only actual snapshot/callback drain completes it,
            including after a later genuine wake following a failure. A permanently broken wake leaves
            it pending and the reactor retains the token/owner callbacks. Requesting removal from a
            callback is allowed; synchronously waiting there is not. ReleaseReference is a separate,
            synchronous owner-worker action after Quiescence, never an implicitly scheduled native job.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.Generation">
            <summary>Gets the immutable connection generation supplied at registration.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.Activation">
            <summary>Gets the nonfaulting, immutable admission result: null on activation, otherwise the initial activation/removal error.</summary>
            <remarks>Readiness may start immediately after activation. Neither success nor failure proves callback drain.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.Quiescence">
            <summary>Gets the nonfaulting barrier completed only after this identity's snapshots and ready/terminal callbacks have drained.</summary>
            <remarks>Does not release the retained reference or prove descriptor close; a broken wake can leave it pending indefinitely.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.RequestRemoval">
            <summary>Publishes removal and returns the stable first wake error, or null; neither outcome acknowledges drain.</summary>
            <remarks>Safe to request within a callback, but never synchronously wait there for <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.Quiescence"/>.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.ReleaseReference">
            <summary>Release exactly once on the native owner worker after Quiescence; never on a reactor thread.</summary>
            <remarks>Early, concurrent, and reactor-thread calls throw. Repeated completed release is a no-op.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadRegistration.Signal">
            <summary>Explicit control wake, not a read callback or a drain acknowledgement; failures throw IOException.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi">
            <summary>Synchronous, borrowed-payload access to unnumbered Linux HID reports.</summary>
            <remarks>
            No method retains a span or owns the caller's buffers. Callers clear sensitive payloads;
            the adapter clears its temporary native framing in finally. Calls may block in native code:
            owners put open, metadata, output, feature I/O and close on their dedicated worker, while
            only nonblocking input belongs on the read reactor. Typed connections enforce FIDO64/OTP8;
            direct report access preserves supported caller lengths. No synthetic report-ID byte escapes.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.Open(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.OpenFlags)">
            <summary>Opens a caller-owned descriptor with close-on-exec, or throws on native failure.</summary>
            <remarks>The owner must drain all borrowers and inspect <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.CloseOnce(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle)"/> before releasing its physical claim.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.GetReportSizes(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle)">
            <summary>Gets bounded input/output payload-size metadata, not actual transfer counts.</summary>
            <remarks>Unavailable descriptor ioctls fall back to 64/64. Invalid sizes and numbered reports fail; parsing is YubiKey-specific.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.TryRead(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.Span{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Attempts one read into borrowed capacity, without padding short input or retrying.</summary>
            <remarks>
            The caller must open nonblocking for reactor use. Only the Data prefix [..ActualBytes]
            is valid; the remainder is unspecified and caller-owned. Capacity is at most 16384 bytes.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.Write(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Writes one payload (0..16383 bytes), adding native report ID zero and requiring the complete framed count.</summary>
            <remarks>Does not require descriptor output length. Failure does not imply the device saw no mutation; no suffix retry is made.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.GetFeature(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.Span{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Gets one eight-byte OTP payload, copying only after an exact nine-byte native transfer with report ID zero.</summary>
            <remarks>Failure leaves the caller destination unchanged; owned native framing is cleared on every exit.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.SetFeature(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Sets one caller-length payload (0..16382 bytes) using zero-ID framing and a length-encoded feature ioctl.</summary>
            <remarks>Requires the complete payload-plus-one native count. Does not retry or impose typed OTP length.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi.CloseOnce(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle)">
            <summary>Requests exactly-once descriptor disposal and observes cached native release evidence.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Returning is not release proof while borrowers or release remain pending. Only Released
            permits claim release; Linux EINTR/EIO preserve diagnostics but prove release, unlike EBADF
            or unknown outcomes. Never retry native close or substitute a timeout for release evidence.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition">
            <summary>Outcome of one native input attempt, independent of descriptor-size metadata.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition.Data">
            <summary>A positive actual payload count is available.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition.WouldBlock">
            <summary>Nonblocking read returned EAGAIN; no payload was delivered.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition.Interrupted">
            <summary>Read returned EINTR; the owner decides whether a bounded retry is allowed.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition.EndOfStream">
            <summary>Read returned zero; the FIDO owner treats this as terminal, not an empty report.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition.Error">
            <summary>Read failed with another captured native error.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadResult">
            <summary>Payload-level result of one input attempt; contains no owned report storage.</summary>
            <param name="Disposition">Classification of the native read outcome.</param>
            <param name="ActualBytes">Valid payload prefix length for Data; zero for every other disposition.</param>
            <param name="Error">Immediately captured errno; meaningful for failed reads, not evidence of failure after success.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadResult.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadDisposition,System.Int32,System.Int32)">
            <summary>Payload-level result of one input attempt; contains no owned report storage.</summary>
            <param name="Disposition">Classification of the native read outcome.</param>
            <param name="ActualBytes">Valid payload prefix length for Data; zero for every other disposition.</param>
            <param name="Error">Immediately captured errno; meaningful for failed reads, not evidence of failure after success.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadResult.Disposition">
            <summary>Classification of the native read outcome.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadResult.ActualBytes">
            <summary>Valid payload prefix length for Data; zero for every other disposition.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxReadResult.Error">
            <summary>Immediately captured errno; meaningful for failed reads, not evidence of failure after success.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidNativeApi">
            <summary>Borrowed synchronous syscall seam beneath report framing, also used by exact ABI/framing tests.</summary>
            <remarks>No method retains buffers. Native counts include any report-ID framing supplied in the buffer.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidNativeApi.Open(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.NativeMethods.OpenFlags)">
            <summary>Acquires a descriptor with the requested flags; returns an invalid handle on failure with errno preserved for immediate capture.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidNativeApi.Read(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.Span{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Performs one read into borrowed storage and captures count/errno before another native call can overwrite them.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidNativeApi.Write(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.ReadOnlySpan{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Performs one write from borrowed native-framed storage and captures count/errno without retry.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidNativeApi.Ioctl(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Native.Linux.Libc.LinuxFileSafeHandle,System.UIntPtr,System.Span{System.Byte})">
            <summary>Performs one ioctl with borrowed request storage; Count is request-specific status or transfer count.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxIoResult">
            <summary>Native return value and errno captured together, with no deferred thread-local error lookup.</summary>
            <param name="Count">Native return: byte count for read/write, ready-descriptor count for poll, request-specific result for ioctl; -1 on failure.</param>
            <param name="Error">Captured errno, meaningful only when the native return indicates failure; it may be stale on success.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxIoResult.#ctor(System.IntPtr,System.Int32)">
            <summary>Native return value and errno captured together, with no deferred thread-local error lookup.</summary>
            <param name="Count">Native return: byte count for read/write, ready-descriptor count for poll, request-specific result for ioctl; -1 on failure.</param>
            <param name="Error">Captured errno, meaningful only when the native return indicates failure; it may be stale on success.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxIoResult.Count">
            <summary>Native return: byte count for read/write, ready-descriptor count for poll, request-specific result for ioctl; -1 on failure.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxIoResult.Error">
            <summary>Captured errno, meaningful only when the native return indicates failure; it may be stale on success.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection">
            <summary>Provides the built-in typed Linux FIDO connection over a hidraw native owner.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Selected by the Linux typed connection factory. Each connection owns a native lifetime with one
                dedicated worker and reactor registration; the synchronous expert facade uses the same owner type,
                not the same owner instance. Only one ordinary send or receive is admitted at a time.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.#ctor(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner)">
            <summary>Takes ownership of an already-open native lifetime.</summary>
            <param name="owner">The owner to shut down when this connection is disposed.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.PacketSize">
            <summary>Gets the 64-byte CTAPHID packet size, excluding the native zero report-ID prefix.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.Type">
            <summary>Gets the FIDO HID transport type.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.OpenAsync(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadReactor,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.IDisposable)">
            <summary>Opens a native lifetime and waits for its input registration to activate.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Native open and metadata operations run on the owner's worker. Cancellation requests shutdown;
                failed or canceled opens await cleanup rather than detaching held native work. The native owner
                accepts the physical claim even on failure and retains it if native release cannot be proven.
            </remarks>
            <param name="path">The hidraw device-node path.</param>
            <param name="reportApi">The borrowed native report seam; this connection does not dispose it.</param>
            <param name="reactor">The borrowed readiness reactor; only this lifetime's registration is removed.</param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Requests cancellation of open and cleanup of acquired native state.</param>
            <param name="physicalClaim">An optional claim transferred to the native owner, not retained by the caller.</param>
            <returns>The caller-owned connection, which must be disposed, preferably asynchronously.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.SendAsync(System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection.SendAsync(System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)" />
            <remarks>
                The owner copies all 64 payload bytes before publishing work to its worker, and clears that copy
                before task completion. Native framing adds a zero report-ID byte. The caller retains its source
                memory and must clear sensitive input after the task is terminal; the SDK never clears that source.
                Cancellation before dispatch skips output; after dispatch the task waits for native completion.
                The caller remains responsible for CTAPHID sequencing and must not interleave session or raw I/O.
            </remarks>
            <param name="packet">Exactly 64 caller-owned payload bytes, without a native report-ID prefix.</param>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancels output only before native dispatch.</param>
            <returns>A task terminal only after any dispatched output returns and its staging copy is cleared.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.ReceiveAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <inheritdoc cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Protocols.Fido.Hid.IFidoHidConnection.ReceiveAsync(System.Threading.CancellationToken)" />
            <remarks>
                Successful receipt transfers an exact-length array to the caller through a read-only view; the
                connection no longer owns or clears it, including on disposal. The view does not make the backing
                storage immutable or provide automatic sensitive-data cleanup. The caller owns its lifetime and
                clearing of sensitive contents. Cancellation detaches only this reader; native input remains active.
                A non-64-byte report terminates input rather than returning a partial typed packet.
                For these built-in array-backed results, use <c>MemoryMarshal.TryGetArray</c> to obtain the owned
                segment, then <c>CryptographicOperations.ZeroMemory(segment.AsSpan())</c> after use. Clearing a
                separate copy does not clear the original. Do not apply this ownership assumption to arbitrary
                custom-provider results without that provider's contract.
            </remarks>
            <param name="cancellationToken">Cancels this pending receive, not the device's protocol exchange.</param>
            <returns>A task yielding 64 caller-owned payload bytes, with no synthetic report-ID prefix.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.RequestTerminalWake">
            <summary>Terminates input and wakes a pending reader without waiting for native release.</summary>
            <remarks>Disposal must still drain the worker and reactor registration and check native close.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.Dispose">
            <summary>Stops admission and synchronously waits for the shared native shutdown outcome.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Blocks through held output, actual reactor quiescence and checked close on the original worker.
                There is no drain timeout; an unproven release retains the owner and any physical claim. Do not call
                from the native worker or read callback. Prefer <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.DisposeAsync" /> to avoid blocking.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.DisposeAsync">
            <summary>Stops admission and asynchronously observes the same native shutdown as <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.Dispose" />.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Completion waits for held output, true reactor quiescence and checked close. A disposal error alone
                is not proof of unreleased state: close diagnostics can be reported after proven release, whereas
                unproven release retains the native owner and any physical claim.
            </remarks>
            <returns>The shared shutdown outcome; it can remain pending indefinitely while native drain is held.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidConnection.Finalize">
            <summary>Requests nonblocking, nonthrowing shutdown without asserting native release.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner">
            <summary>Owns the shared native lifetime behind typed Linux FIDO and direct synchronous IO-report access.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Roots the owner before native acquisition and retains the original physical claim through
            cleanup. One dedicated worker owns open, metadata, output, activation/drain waits and checked
            close; the reactor only performs bounded nonblocking input. Send and receive share one ordinary
            operation slot with no backlog. Terminal tasks are not native-release evidence: only actual
            registration quiescence followed by checked close permits claim release. Unknown release retains
            and quarantines the rooted lifetime, without a replacement worker, close retry or timeout escape.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.ReportCapacity">
            <summary>Maximum queued owned reports; overflow terminates input rather than silently dropping frames.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.InputReportSize">
            <summary>Gets bounded descriptor input payload capacity (1..4096), not the actual length of a received report.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.OutputReportSize">
            <summary>Gets bounded descriptor output metadata (1..4096); raw sends need not match this length.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.OpenAsync(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReadReactor,System.Threading.CancellationToken,System.IDisposable)">
            <summary>Creates a rooted owner and opens/registers its nonblocking descriptor on the original dedicated worker.</summary>
            <param name="path">hidraw path to open.</param>
            <param name="api">Borrowed synchronous report seam; not disposed by this owner.</param>
            <param name="reactor">Borrowed readiness service; only this owner's registration is removed on shutdown.</param>
            <param name="token">Cancels before acquisition or requests shutdown during preparation; cannot detach a submitted native call.</param>
            <param name="claim">Optional original physical/discovery claim transferred to this lifetime, including failed-open cleanup.</param>
            <returns>The prepared owner after metadata validation and registration activation.</returns>
            <remarks>
            Failed/canceled open awaits cleanup and preserves both open and cleanup failures. Activation
            failure still retains its token until actual drain. Held native work or broken wake can leave
            this task pending indefinitely; caller cancellation is not permission to release the claim.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.SendAsync(System.ReadOnlyMemory{System.Byte},System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>Admits one raw send and copies its payload before returning the actual operation task.</summary>
            <param name="report">Caller-owned payload (0..16383 bytes), not required to match metadata; typed callers enforce 64 bytes.</param>
            <param name="token">Checked at entry and worker dispatch; after dispatch it cannot detach or abort output.</param>
            <returns>A task terminal only after dispatched output returns and the owned copy is cleared, or pre-dispatch cancellation/failure.</returns>
            <remarks>
            Admission takes the owner lock and rejects overlap. The caller retains and clears its source;
            the worker borrows only the owned copy. Native failure is not replayed or proof of no mutation.
            Successful output may succeed despite cancellation requested after dispatch.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.ReceiveAsync(System.Boolean,System.Threading.CancellationToken)">
            <summary>Admits one reader for the next exact-length owned report, consuming queued input when available.</summary>
            <param name="typed">Whether delivery must contain exactly 64 bytes; a mismatch clears the report and terminalizes input.</param>
            <param name="token">Checked at entry; cancellation of a pending reader detaches only that reader, not native input.</param>
            <returns>The reader's actual completion task, yielding caller-owned array storage through a read-only view.</returns>
            <remarks>
            Raw input preserves actual read length rather than padding to descriptor capacity. Delivery
            races cancellation exactly once; undelivered reports remain available for a successor. The
            owner clears temporary, rejected and abandoned queued reports, but never a successfully returned
            array, including during shutdown. The view offers no disposal/zeroing API; read-only does not
            mean immutable or automatically erased. Sensitive successful output requires caller-managed
            backing-storage cleanup; copying it does not erase the original. Continuations run asynchronously.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.Terminal(System.Exception)">
            <summary>Stops input/admission, clears queued reports and faults a pending reader with the first terminal cause.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Requests registration removal without waiting. Does not abort accepted output, close the
            descriptor, release the claim or prove quiescence; <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.ShutdownAsync"/> still owns cleanup.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.Dispose">
            <summary>Synchronously drains the same checked shutdown outcome used by asynchronous disposal.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Retained for the synchronous expert facade and IDisposable compatibility, not the async
            golden path. Prefer awaiting <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.ShutdownAsync"/>. This can block indefinitely on native
            work or true reactor drain. Worker/read-callback and all reactor-thread calls are rejected to
            prevent self-drain; do not invoke while holding a resource needed by the operation being drained.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.RequestShutdown">
            <summary>Requests shutdown for facade finalization without waiting for native cleanup or propagating exceptions.</summary>
            <remarks>Takes the admission lock; this is not a lock-free request or release acknowledgement.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxFidoHidNativeOwner.ShutdownAsync">
            <summary>Closes admission, terminalizes a pending reader and returns the shared checked native-cleanup outcome.</summary>
            <remarks>
            Requests removal even during held output. The original worker finishes accepted work, waits
            for the exact registration's actual quiescence, releases its reference and inspects checked
            close before releasing the original claim. No timeout frees a live lifetime. Released EINTR/EIO
            close still faults with diagnostics after claim release; unproven release faults while retaining
            the root/claim in quarantine. Repeated calls share the result and never retry close. Public read
            terminality precedes cleanup, but does not itself prove native quiescence or close.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener">
            <summary>
            Linux implementation of HID device listener using udev_monitor with poll().
            </summary>
            <remarks>
            <para>
            The listener does not auto-start. Call <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start"/> after setting up
            <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.HidDeviceListener.Changed"/> callback.
            </para>
            <para>
            Each <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start"/> creates an independent session that owns its native resources
            (udev context, monitor, shutdown event fd). The session's listener thread disposes those
            resources when its loop exits. If <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Stop"/> times out waiting for a wedged thread,
            the cancelled session is abandoned rather than torn down, so a late-exiting thread can never
            touch recycled file descriptors or state belonging to a newer session.
            </para>
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.#ctor">
            <summary>
            Creates a new instance. The listener does not start automatically - call <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start"/>
            after setting up the <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.HidDeviceListener.Changed"/> callback.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.#ctor(System.Func{Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource})">
            <summary>
            Creates a new instance with a custom event source factory. Used by tests to inject a
            fake <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource"/> for no-hardware fault injection.
            </summary>
            <param name="eventSourceFactory">
            Factory invoked once per <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start"/> to create the session's event source,
            or null to use the udev-backed production source.
            </param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Stop">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.SetErrorStatus(Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.ListenerSession)">
            <summary>
            Transitions the listener to <see cref="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.DeviceListenerStatus.Error"/> unless the session
            was cancelled, so an abandoned (zombie) session can never stamp Error onto a newer session.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.ListenerSession">
            <summary>
            Per-<see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener.Start"/> state: the event source, its listener thread, and the
            cancellation flag. Sessions are never reused; a session's thread is the sole owner of
            its source's disposal.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome">
            <summary>
            Outcome of a single blocking wait on the Linux HID event source.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.Retry">
            <summary>No actionable outcome (EINTR or spurious wake); poll again.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.Event">
            <summary>The udev monitor has at least one event ready to receive.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.ShutdownSignaled">
            <summary>The shutdown event fd was signaled; exit the loop cleanly.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.ShutdownFdError">
            <summary>The shutdown event fd reported an error condition; the loop cannot be woken reliably.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.MonitorFdError">
            <summary>The udev monitor fd reported an error condition; events can no longer be received.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="F:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidPollOutcome.PollFailed">
            <summary>poll() failed with a non-EINTR error.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidUdevEvent">
            <summary>
            A udev event received from the Linux HID event source, reduced to the fields the
            listener needs to construct a <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.HidDeviceRescanHint"/>.
            </summary>
            <param name="Action">The udev action string ("add", "remove", ...), or null when unavailable.</param>
            <param name="StableIdentity">The stable udev/sysfs identity (parent HID device syspath), or null.</param>
            <param name="DevNode">The /dev/hidrawN node path, diagnostic only, or null.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidUdevEvent.#ctor(System.String,System.String,System.String)">
            <summary>
            A udev event received from the Linux HID event source, reduced to the fields the
            listener needs to construct a <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.HidDeviceRescanHint"/>.
            </summary>
            <param name="Action">The udev action string ("add", "remove", ...), or null when unavailable.</param>
            <param name="StableIdentity">The stable udev/sysfs identity (parent HID device syspath), or null.</param>
            <param name="DevNode">The /dev/hidrawN node path, diagnostic only, or null.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidUdevEvent.Action">
            <summary>The udev action string ("add", "remove", ...), or null when unavailable.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidUdevEvent.StableIdentity">
            <summary>The stable udev/sysfs identity (parent HID device syspath), or null.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidUdevEvent.DevNode">
            <summary>The /dev/hidrawN node path, diagnostic only, or null.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource">
            <summary>
            Abstraction over the native udev monitor, poll(), and shutdown eventfd used by
            <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidDeviceListener"/>. Enables no-hardware fault-injection tests of the
            listener's loop policy (bounded exit, error transitions, hint emission).
            </summary>
            <remarks>
            Ownership: the listener session's thread owns the source and disposes it when the loop
            exits. <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.SignalShutdown"/> may be called concurrently from other threads and
            must tolerate a source that is concurrently disposing.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.Initialize">
            <summary>
            Creates the udev context, monitor (hidraw subsystem filter, receiving enabled), and
            shutdown event fd. Returns false when any native step fails; the caller disposes the
            source in that case.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.WaitForEvent">
            <summary>
            Blocks until the monitor has an event, the shutdown fd is signaled, or an error occurs.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.ReceiveEvent">
            <summary>
            Receives one pending udev event. Returns null when the receive fails — which is most
            likely during event storms (for example ENOBUFS after the kernel dropped notifications),
            exactly when a removal may have been lost.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.SignalShutdown">
            <summary>
            Wakes a thread blocked in <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.WaitForEvent"/> so it can observe cancellation.
            Safe to call from any thread, including concurrently with <see cref="M:System.IDisposable.Dispose"/>.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource.IsHidrawReady(System.String)">
            <summary>
            Returns true when the hidraw device node exists and can be opened, indicating the node
            is ready for enumeration by discovery rescans.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource">
            <summary>
            Production <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidEventSource"/> backed by libudev and libc P/Invoke.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.Initialize">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.WaitForEvent">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.ReceiveEvent">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.SignalShutdown">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.IsHidrawReady(System.String)">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.Dispose">
            <inheritdoc />
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxUdevHidEventSource.TrimHidrawSyspath(System.String)">
            <summary>
            Trims the hidraw-specific suffix from a hidraw syspath so the remaining prefix
            identifies the parent HID device stably across hidraw node renumbering.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection">
            <summary>Synchronous expert feature-report facade over the Linux OTP native lifetime.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Each instance owns a separate native lifetime whose dedicated worker performs open, GET, SET and
                checked close. GET uses the eight-byte OTP payload contract; direct SET preserves supported caller
                lengths instead of imposing typed OTP8. Native framing supplies a zero report-ID byte not exposed in
                payloads. The caller owns protocol framing, sequencing and recovery and must not interleave session I/O.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.#ctor(System.String)">
            <summary>Synchronously opens hidraw feature-report access using the production native seam.</summary>
            <param name="devNode">The hidraw device-node path.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.#ctor(System.String,Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.ILinuxHidReportApi)">
            <summary>Synchronously waits for the dedicated worker to open hidraw feature-report access.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Does not initialize an OTP protocol or change process-wide settings. Failed open waits for cleanup
                of any acquired native state; neither construction nor its cleanup has a timeout.
            </remarks>
            <param name="devNode">The hidraw device-node path.</param>
            <param name="api">The borrowed native report seam; this connection does not dispose it.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.InputReportSize">
            <summary>Gets the eight-byte GET payload size, excluding the native zero report-ID byte.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.OutputReportSize">
            <summary>Gets the nominal eight-byte OTP output payload size, excluding the native zero report ID.</summary>
            <remarks>This metadata does not constrain direct <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.SetReport(System.Byte[])" /> calls to eight bytes.</remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.Type">
            <summary>Gets the generic HID type retained by this untyped report facade.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.GetReport">
            <summary>Synchronously receives an eight-byte OTP feature payload into newly allocated storage.</summary>
            <remarks>
                The caller supplies no destination buffer. The worker allocates eight bytes; the native seam uses
                nine framed bytes and strips the leading zero report ID. Native framing and failed/partial owner
                output are cleared. This built-in implementation transfers the exact array to the caller without
                another copy; its array signature preserves <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.IHidConnection" /> compatibility. The caller
                must clear sensitive returned contents after use; connection disposal does not clear them. This call waits for native
                GET to return, without a timeout. Only one ordinary GET or SET is admitted at a time.
            </remarks>
            <returns>A caller-owned eight-byte payload array without the native report-ID prefix.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.SetReport(System.Byte[])">
            <summary>Synchronously sends a feature payload, preserving its supplied length.</summary>
            <remarks>
                The array signature deliberately preserves <see cref="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.IHidConnection" /> compatibility. The owner
                copies the payload before publishing work to its worker and clears that copy before completion.
                The native seam adds a zero report-ID prefix and clears its own framing storage. This call blocks
                until native SET returns, but never clears or takes ownership of the caller's source array. Keep
                the source stable during the call and clear sensitive contents afterward. Length need not equal
                <see cref="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.OutputReportSize" />: up to 16382 payload bytes are forwarded, subject to native/device
                support. Overlapping ordinary GET or SET calls are rejected.
            </remarks>
            <param name="report">The caller-owned payload, without a native report-ID prefix.</param>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.Dispose">
            <summary>Stops admission and blocks for the shared native shutdown outcome.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Waits for any admitted GET/SET to return and checked close on the original worker. There is no drain
                timeout; unproven release retains the native owner. Calling from the native worker is rejected.
                Prefer <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.DisposeAsync" /> to avoid blocking the caller.
            </remarks>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.DisposeAsync">
            <summary>Stops admission and asynchronously observes the same native shutdown as <see cref="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.Dispose" />.</summary>
            <remarks>
                Waits for admitted feature I/O and checked close. Close diagnostics can fault disposal after proven
                release; unproven release retains the native owner instead.
            </remarks>
            <returns>The shared shutdown outcome, potentially pending indefinitely while a native call is held.</returns>
        </member>
        <member name="M:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidFeatureReportConnection.Finalize">
            <summary>Requests nonblocking, nonthrowing shutdown without waiting for or asserting native release.</summary>
        </member>
        <member name="T:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidInterface">
            <summary>
                Linux implementation of a Human Interface Device (HID) using hidraw.
            </summary>
        </member>
        <member name="P:Yubico.YubiKit.Core.Transports.Hid.Linux.LinuxHidInterface.DescriptorInfo">
